From: Project Luma <builds@project-luma.local>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 18:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] luma: Dash islands as arranged, on every edge and setting

The owner's findings on .118 (ADR-044):

- The live extensions glyph ships: luma-live-extensions-symbolic, a
  fills-only symbolic in the Shell's hicolor icons (0171 named it and
  nothing provided it).
- Centre means centre: a centred group stays on the monitor's centre line;
  when it would meet the groups at the ends, the dock gives up length on
  both sides alike before anything moves.
- Start and end reach the true ends of every edge: a side band stops short
  of a corner only when a top or bottom band is there.
- Free placement off: exactly the three zones per edge and nothing else.
  On: no zone grid, one faint line per edge; an island lands exactly where
  it is dropped, with soft snaps (12 px) at the ends and centres and an
  attach right beside another island.
- Attach or apart: an attach within 20 px of an island's end, apart from
  56 px, and a dead zone between where the preview does not change; the
  chosen target is kept until another is better by 24 px, and targets are
  measured against the layout as it was when the drag began, never the
  preview, so nothing jitters or swaps.
- Live extensions are one family: the live island and what is playing sit
  side by side in one slot and move together; split placements are joined
  where the first one was.
- Hidden islands take no length and hold no edge: an edge whose only
  islands are hidden (notifications with nothing unread, no live activity)
  reserves nothing, and is reserved again when one shows. Arrange mode
  shows a ghost in each hidden slot (Live extensions, Notifications).
  An empty edge reserves nothing at all (no margin strut, no work-area
  inset), so tiles and maximized windows reach the screen there; windows
  keep the padding only from a band, or from every edge with Reserve work
  area off. Each change of what an edge reserves is logged for Vitals
  (LUMA_SHELF_RESERVATIONS), per monitor and edge.
- Use islands off: one bar the whole length of each occupied edge, its
  groups laid out inside it by the band solver exactly as arranged, with a
  divider between its parts, on all four edges alike. With islands, Span
  full edge lets the end groups reach the screen's ends (Float off); no
  island changes length, so arrange mode shows what the desktop shows.
- One divider token, 2 px and the island's full height, for Quick Options'
  parts and a bar's parts, in every mode.
- Notifications come from their island: a click on the island opens the
  notification drawer (and closes it again) instead of the newest
  notification; the drawer opens on the island's inner side, and urgent
  cards sit beside the island on its monitor, both moving in from the
  island's edge, wherever it is placed.
- A carried island keeps the point where it was grabbed when it turns into
  the other form (a column on a side edge).
.121, the owner's findings on .120:

- Every edge's ends reach the corner: a side band stops short of one only
  when something of the top or bottom band is at that end (a group anchored
  there, or a bar, which runs its whole edge). At a corner two zone
  placeholders overlap; the one whose centre is nearest the pointer wins.
- Use islands off: a top or bottom bar runs into the corner and the side
  bar starts just past it, square where they meet and with no rim across
  the joint, so the two read as one L with no gap, overlap or doubled line.
- The group handle is a 32 x 6 pill in a 48 x 22 target that says what it
  does on hover; it moves the whole group, while an island dragged by
  itself still moves alone (the live family together).
- Arrange mode shows only the Live extensions and Notifications
  placeholders, always in their own slot, and never a live extension, what
  is playing or a real notification.
- An item with nothing to show takes no length, no spacing, no separation
  and no divider, as a group of its own too; it opens when it becomes
  visible (the live island and the Well grow in as the notifications island
  does) and the notifications island holds its slot only while something is
  waiting.
- Hovering the notifications island changes only its look: no actions are
  swapped in, and a press whose release went elsewhere never leaves it
  following the pointer.
- Dropping into a group opens the slot under the pointer: the index is the
  number of islands whose midpoint the pointer has passed, measured as they
  sit without the preview's slot and without the carried island, so it is
  right at both ends, for start, centre and end groups, on every edge.
- Tiling Shell's panel button is never adopted into the Dash.
- Windows follow the Dash: before it changes what it reserves it emits
  work-area-changing with Main.shelf.workAreaNext, and Main.shelf.
  workAreaMotion carries the Dash's own duration and curve (250 ms,
  ease-out-quint, 0 with reduced motion). Main.wm.lumaGlideFrame moves and
  resizes a window in one continuous glide with no cross-fade, so Tiling
  Shell's re-tile runs with the island's animation instead of after it.

